.TH "MON" "1" "" "mon"
.SH NAME
mon \- react to changes in a file system path
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]mon\f[R] \-c \f[I]COMMAND\f[R] \f[I]<PATH/PATTERN>\f[R]
.SH EXAMPLES
\f[B]mon\f[R] \-c \f[I]\[lq]make test\[rq]\f[R] \f[I]/path/to/dir\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]mon\f[R] \-c \f[I]\[lq]make test\[rq]\f[R] \f[I]/path/to/dir/*\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]mon\f[R] \-c \f[I]\[lq]make test\[rq]\f[R] \f[I]$(find .
\-iname *.c \-o \-iname *.h)\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]mon\f[R] Is a utility program that can trigger user provided shell
commands when file system changes occur in a provided directory path.
It can be useful if you want to trigger tests automatically while
developing some code.
Or automatically move new files once created.
The main goal is to do it\[cq]s job through a simple command line
interface and not require any deep understanding of file system events.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R]
Print help
.TP
\f[B]\-c\f[R] \f[I]COMMAND\f[R]
A command to run when a change is detected.
Can be provided multiple times.
.TP
\f[B]\-C\f[R]
Clear the terminal before executing the commands.
Equivalent to `\f[B]\-c\f[R] \f[I]clear\f[R]' as a first command.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\-V\f[R]
Verbose (\f[I]\-v\f[R]) and extra verbose (\f[I]\-V\f[R]) output
